        "handle": "Drew Willcoxon",
        "img_url": "https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/308b00b42f1a2f3c374bb3a7a851c115?size=240&default=https%3A%2F%2Fs3-alpha.figma.com%2Fstatic%2Fuser_d_v2.png",
        "id": "950935078959088945"
      },
      "created_at": "2025-06-26T19:01:25.516Z",
      "resolved_at": null,
      "message": "Some users skip versions, or upgrade days/weeks after a new version is released. Instead of basing the starting point for this logic on the version that the feature was introduced in, should it be based on the first time the user sees the New label for that feature? So f.e. if a feature is introduced in 150 but the user skips to 152, they won't see the label at all if we base the calculation on 150. If instead we base it on the first time they saw the label, in 152, then we can continue to show the label for 2 versions, in 152 and 153.\n\nWhat if the user is new to Firefox? Should they see the New label, since everything is new to them?\n\nThe bullet point here says \"visible for 2 versions (8 weeks)\" -- should the calculation be based on number of versions or time elapsed? Or can engineering pick?\n\nOne bullet point here says 2 versions but the next point says 3 versions max -- so is it really 3 versions max? For clarity, could we remove the \"2 versions\" wording?",
      "reactions": [],
